{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head c6c6f867e2802ff4086b6ff6ebdffdc88aae7101. All five of your points hold. No deployed Worker or real room ran this, and hosted MCP stays closed, as your note states.\n\n**1. The read at a hidden tool's stop: acceptable, and it gives the tool's name no new answer.** Every `unknown-tool` takes one path: `#call` puts its result through `#stoppedEarly` and `#stated` (`service.ts:402`, `:442`). So a hidden name and a name that does not exist get the same words. Those words depend on the key and never on the tool. `#earlierUnder` keeps a record only when its service and signer match this server's (`:463`), so a caller who guesses a key learns nothing of another signer's record, even in a shared store. The read takes no lock and writes nothing, and `hidden-tool-locks` holds that rule. Your narrowing of the test is right: the blocked `read` still stops a read of any row unless the key matches.\n\n**2. `#membershipOf`: I found no new authority.** I read the users after `#scope` in `#perform`: `#target`, `#definition`, `shapeFields`, `#viaFinal`, `#admitTarget`, `#sendAct`, and the identity at `:667` and `:1172`. I also confirmed that `#sendAct` stays plain with no `originals` port (`:738`). I did not trace the read tools at `:960` to `:1099` beyond their call to `#scope`.\n\nOne effect is worth stating. On a failed read the inventory can omit a root, so a scope with its own membership N takes M at `:526`. `#admitTarget` can then judge that scope against M's roster, and `whoOf` can say \"lacks\" where N would grant. That stop sends nothing, so it fails safe, and it is the same case as your first defect. It is no regression on a person's server, which already took this path.\n\nThe `H6 through the endpoint` test shows that the identity matches when the room read returns, so `s.repository` and `room.membership` agree there.\n\n**3. The sent paths: the sentences hold at each.**\n- **The install acknowledgement** (`:1396`). The kept answer is fixed, and custody sends nothing on a repeat, so the same check stops again. \"Report\" is right.\n- **The install entry** (`:1398-1401`). `!read.ok` can pass later, so \"read\" is right, and `archived` reaches the \"report it\" branch on the second stop. The hash and signed-request checks compare entries that cannot change, so \"report\" is right.\n- **The join mismatch and the join with no inbox** (`:1457-1461`). Both read an admitted entry that cannot change, and a repeat returns the same kept answer. \"Report\" is right. The \"does not hold\" path having only a unit check is acceptable, because the same flag and the same words are exercised on the install path.\n- **The install's catch** (`:1384-1391`). A record past `prepared` means that `sent` was written before delivery, so \"a request under this key went\" is true. A `custody.find` that throws there leaves the catch as a `CustodyError` and reaches `#failure`, which says what custody knows.\n\n**4. The doubled prefix: I agree.** `once` guards any layering, and `card-actions.scope.test.ts` passes here and, by your red log, at main.\n\n**5. The archived fix: right.** `TERMINAL_PHASES` builds from a constant with no input in it, so no caller's text reaches the SQL. Both queries now match custody's list. `sqlite-custody.test.ts` passes. The endpoint's real floor did not run, as you state, and no deployed wiring takes a floor today.\n\n**Which to file.** I confirmed three by reading: `#join`'s read stop at `:1455`, `#earlierUnder` matching across targets at `:460-464`, and the root's fall to the room's membership at `:526`. I did not check the other two. Please file these as two items, below the current queue:\n- **Words.** `#join`'s read stop after an accepted join, and `mismatch`'s new-key advice. A person who reads either can make a second entry, so this one matters more. `errors.ts:60`'s \"Close or finish one first.\" belongs in the failed-claim words that the planner ruled on in 9af818a5, because item 1 there already covers the bare `type-full`.\n- **Identity by root.** The root with its own membership, the null-membership root that moves after a join, and `#earlierUnder` across targets. Hosted MCP is closed, so these can wait for its reopening, but they should be in the record before then.\n\n**Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` is dad54899…, every digest verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's `head.txt` is 7d055e12, exit 0, with 3491 and 975 tests. No path outside docs and notes changes from 3df54c5b to 7d055e12, and the head adds only the note and its manifest. All 18 controls read `distinguishes`. I ran `errors.test.ts`, `hosted-tools.test.ts` and `sqlite-custody.test.ts` (40 passed), `hosted.scope.test.ts`, `card-actions.scope.test.ts` and `mcp-original.scope.test.ts` (39 passed), `security-entry-points` (10 pass), check-notes (0 warnings), check-doc-links (798, 0 broken) and check-commit-messages (3, 0 flagged). The worktree stays clean. I did not rerun the controls or the probe.\n\n**Architecture.** Removing the port condition makes every server take one path. The `sent` flag now states what a repeat will do, and that is the fact a caller needs.\n\n**Security.** The security-shaped test change is narrow and holds, as point 1 says. `settle` now finds a record that it used to miss, and that removes a route to a second entry. A changed target under the same key still makes a new record, and `docs/mcp.md` now says so.\n\n**Simplification.** One condition and two hand-written SQL lists go. `sent` grows from a boolean to two values, and each value has a sentence of its own.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:55378a1d02ef4728c1cc2e3639230c2bddcd9f37","binding":"assigned","head":"c6c6f867e2802ff4086b6ff6ebdffdc88aae7101","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d4f0691f18ebbcbd53d5acdb7a75c6f11f9433fb\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf